## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical integration of fetch, Service Worker offline caching, and WebSocket real-time streaming, plus a minimal DOM patch engine. It also exercises offline-first UX with a replay queue and robust reconnect/backoff.

## Key Concepts
- Cache API / Service Worker lifecycle
- WebSocket reconnect with exponential backoff
- Minimal DOM patching / diffing
- Ordering by timestamp and dedup by id
- Offline queue and replay

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal fetch wrapper
async function fetchWithCache(url, opts = {}) {
  const cacheKey = url;
  const cache = await caches.open('liveinfo-cache');
  // Try cache first
  const cached = await cache.match(cacheKey);
  if (cached) {
    const text = await cached.text();
    try { return JSON.parse(text); } catch { /* fallthrough */ }
  }
  // Fallback to network
  const res = await fetch(url, opts);
  if (res.ok) {
    const clone = res.clone();
    cache.put(cacheKey, clone);
    return res.json();
  }
  throw new Error('Network error');
}
```
```javascript
// Minimal WebSocket wrapper
function createWebSocket(url, onMessage) {
  let ws;
  let delay = 1000;
  let stopped = false;
  const connect = () => {
    if (stopped) return;
    ws = new WebSocket(url);
    ws.addEventListener('message', (e) => onMessage(JSON.parse(e.data)));
    ws.addEventListener('close', () => {
      if (stopped) return;
      setTimeout(connect, delay);
      delay = Math.min(delay * 2, 30000);
    });
  };
  connect();
  return {
    close() { stopped = true; ws && ws.close(); },
    send(d) { ws && ws.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN && ws.send(JSON.stringify(d)); }
  };
}
```
```javascript
// Minimal DOM patcher
class PatchStore {
  constructor(root) {
    this.root = root;
    this.items = new Map();
  }
  patch(update) {
    const { id, ts, ...rest } = update;
    const existing = this.items.get(id);
    if (!existing || ts >= existing.ts) {
      this.items.set(id, { ts, ...rest });
    }
  }
  render() {
    // naive render: rebuilds list in order
    const list = [...this.items.values()].sort((a,b)=> a.ts - b.ts);
    this.root.innerHTML = '';
    for (const it of list) {
      const el = document.createElement('div');
      el.textContent = `${it.id}: ${it.value} @ ${new Date(it.ts).toLocaleTimeString()}`;
      this.root.appendChild(el);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply progressive hydration, per-column lazy-loading, and worker offloading to meet Lighthouse budgets in a heavy dashboard. Also probes SSR/CSR trade-offs and how to validate improvements.

## Key Concepts

- Progressive hydration
- Dynamic imports and code-splitting
- Virtualization
- Web Workers
- Lighthouse budgets and metrics
- IntersectionObserver for loading offscreen content

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example skeleton: dynamic import of heavy cell renderer
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';
const HeavyCell = dynamic(() => import('./cells/HeavyCell'));

// Progressive hydration with IntersectionObserver
const useColumnHydration = (columnRef) => {
  const [isHydrated, setIsHydrated] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      ([entry]) => {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
          setIsHydrated(true);
          observer.disconnect();
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0.1 }
    );

    if (columnRef.current) {
      observer.observe(columnRef.current);
    }

    return () => observer.disconnect();
  }, []);

  return isHydrated;
};
```

